
PuTTY Power User Quick Guide


📂 1. Save Your Session

Stop re-typing your IP address every time you connect.
• Configure: Enter your IP address, Port 22, and select SSH.
• Name: Go to Saved Sessions, type a nickname (e.g., Production-Server).
• Save: Click the Save button.
• Launch: Double-click the saved name next time to connect instantly.

📝 2. Turn on Session Logging

Automatically save everything printed on your terminal screen to a local text file.
• Navigate: Go to Session ➔ Logging in the left menu.
• Enable: Select All session output.
• Path: Click Browse next to Log file name to choose where to save the .log file on your computer.
• Lock it in: Go back to Session, select your saved session, and click Save.

🔑 3. Set Up Passwordless SSH Keys

Log in instantly and securely without typing a password.

Part A: Generate the Keys

• Run: Open PuTTYgen (installed alongside PuTTY).
• Create: Click Generate and wiggle your mouse in the blank space.
• Save Private: Click Save private key (saves as a .ppk file). Keep this file safe and private.
• Copy Public: Copy the long text block in the box at the top starting with ssh-rsa.

Part B: Authorize on Server

• Log In: Connect to your server with your regular password one last time.
• Edit File: Run nano ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
• Paste: Paste the copied public key text onto a new line. Save and exit (Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X).

Part C: Link to PuTTY

• Load Key: In PuTTY, go to Connection ➔ SSH ➔ Auth ➔ Credentials.
• Browse: Select your saved .ppk private key file.
• Save Session: Go back to Session, select your saved profile, and click Save.
